/* Lesson quizzes — batch 15 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p41m1":[
 {id:"p41m1q1", q:"A qubit sits on the Bloch sphere at polar angle θ = 60° and azimuth φ = 90°. Measured in the computational basis, what is the probability of reading 0?", options:["0.25","0.87","0.75","0.50"], answer:2, why:"P(0) = cos²(θ/2) = cos²(30°) = 0.75. The azimuth φ is a relative phase and leaves computational-basis probabilities unchanged; 0.87 is cos 30° without squaring, and 0.25 is P(1)."},
 {id:"p41m1q2", q:"Two qubits are each secretly prepared in |+⟩ or |−⟩. Measured directly, both give 50/50 counts. Which procedure tells you, with certainty and in a single shot each, which state each qubit holds?", options:["Apply a Hadamard, then measure: |+⟩ reads 0 and |−⟩ reads 1","Measure 10,000 shots each and compare the exact 0/1 ratios","None can: states with equal probabilities are the same state","Apply a global phase of −1 and watch for a change in counts"], answer:0, why:"H maps |+⟩ to |0⟩ and |−⟩ to |1⟩, because the relative sign makes the amplitudes add or cancel. More shots only sharpen the same 50/50 ratio: equal probabilities do not mean equal states, and a global phase is unobservable."},
 {id:"p41m1q3", q:"Grover's algorithm is applied to the 2¹²⁸ possible keys of AES-128. Roughly how many cipher evaluations does it need?", options:["About 128, one per key bit","About 2⁶⁴","About 2¹²⁷, no better than brute force","About 2³²"], answer:1, why:"Grover needs about √N evaluations, and √(2¹²⁸) = 2⁶⁴: a quadratic speedup, not an exponential one. That is why AES-256 keeps about 128-bit security against quantum attack, while Shor, not Grover, is what breaks RSA and elliptic curves."},
 {id:"p41m1q4", q:"You apply two Hadamards to qubit 0 and measure, 4,000 shots on a real IBM processor. Counts: 3,958 zeros and 42 ones. What best explains the ones?", options:["Interference only works on simulators; hardware returns random bits","The second Hadamard re-creates a superposition, so some ones are expected","Too few shots: a million shots would give exactly zero ones","About 1% hardware error, mostly in readout, flips a few results"], answer:3, why:"H·H = I, so the ideal result is all zeros, which a simulator shows. About 1% ones matches current readout and gate error. Random output would be near 2,000 each, and more shots never remove a physical error rate."},
 {id:"p41m1q5", q:"A client asks which workload a future fault-tolerant quantum computer would most plausibly speed up. Which is the best candidate?", options:["Their PostgreSQL order database and web front end","Ground-state energies of a candidate battery molecule","Exact polynomial-time solutions to large SAT scheduling instances","Sorting and deduplicating 10 TB of log files every night"], answer:1, why:"Simulating quantum systems is exponentially costly classically but polynomial for many cases on a quantum machine, Feynman's original motivation. NP-complete problems such as SAT are expected to get only Grover's square-root gain, and ordinary software gains nothing."}
],
"p41m2":[
 {id:"p41m2q1", q:"A two-qubit state gives P(00) = 0.4, P(01) = 0.1, P(10) = 0.2 and P(11) = 0.3. What is the expectation value ⟨Z⊗Z⟩?", options:["0.7","0.1","0.4","−0.4"], answer:2, why:"Z⊗Z gives +1 when the bits agree and −1 when they differ: (0.4 + 0.3) − (0.1 + 0.2) = 0.7 − 0.3 = 0.4. Answering 0.7 counts only the agreeing outcomes and forgets to subtract the disagreeing ones."},
 {id:"p41m2q2", q:"A 3-qubit Qiskit circuit applies X only to qubit 2, leaving qubits 0 and 1 in |0⟩. Which count key appears, and which statevector index holds the amplitude 1?", options:["'100', at index 4","'001', at index 1","'100', at index 1","'001', at index 4"], answer:0, why:"Qiskit is little-endian: qubit 0 is the rightmost character, so qubit 2 is the leftmost and the key is '100'. The statevector index is that string read as binary, 4. '001' is the left-to-right reading the lecture warns against."},
 {id:"p41m2q3", q:"The state (1/√2)|00⟩ + (1/2)|10⟩ + (1/2)|11⟩ is written |q₁q₀⟩. You measure only qubit 0 and get 0. With what probability, and what is the state afterwards?", options:["1/2; the state becomes |00⟩","1/4; the state becomes |11⟩","3/4; (1/√2)|00⟩ + (1/2)|10⟩, unchanged","3/4; √(2/3)|00⟩ + (1/√3)|10⟩"], answer:3, why:"Qubit 0 is the right digit, so |00⟩ and |10⟩ survive: P = 1/2 + 1/4 = 3/4. Dividing each amplitude by √(3/4) renormalizes them to √(2/3) and 1/√3. The 1/2 answer measured the left digit, qubit 1, instead."},
 {id:"p41m2q4", q:"Which of these matrices could be implemented as a single-qubit gate on a quantum computer?", options:["(1/√2)[[1,1],[1,1]]","[[1,0],[0,i]]","[[1,1],[0,1]]","(1/2)[[1,1],[1,−1]]"], answer:1, why:"A gate must satisfy U†U = I. diag(1, i) is the S gate and passes. (1/√2)[[1,1],[1,1]] is singular, [[1,1],[0,1]] gives U†U = [[1,1],[1,2]], and (1/2)[[1,1],[1,−1]] gives I/2: a Hadamard with the wrong normalization."},
 {id:"p41m2q5", q:"Ignoring overhead, how many qubits can a workstation with 64 GiB of RAM simulate as an exact complex128 statevector?", options:["32","36","64","30"], answer:0, why:"Each amplitude takes 16 bytes, so 2ⁿ × 16 B ≤ 2³⁶ B gives 2ⁿ ≤ 2³², n = 32. Answering 36 forgets the 16 bytes per amplitude; every extra qubit doubles the memory, so 64 qubits would need 256 EiB."}
],
"p41m3":[
 {id:"p41m3q1", q:"A qubit starts in |0⟩. You apply H, then Z, then H, and measure in the computational basis. What do you get?", options:["0 with certainty, because Z is invisible to measurement","0 or 1 with 50% each","0 with certainty, because H·H cancels","1 with certainty, because HZH equals X"], answer:3, why:"H turns |0⟩ into |+⟩, Z flips it to |−⟩, and the second H maps |−⟩ to |1⟩: HZH = X. Z is invisible only to a measurement made directly after it; between Hadamards the phase flip becomes a bit flip."},
 {id:"p41m3q2", q:"Starting from |00⟩, a Qiskit circuit runs qc.x(0); qc.cx(0, 1); qc.cx(1, 0) and then measures both qubits. Which count key results?", options:["'01'","'10'","'11'","'00'"], answer:1, why:"X sets qubit 0: '01'. CX(0,1) sees control 1 and flips qubit 1: '11'. CX(1,0) sees qubit 1 = 1 and flips qubit 0 back: '10'. In little-endian keys qubit 1 is the left character, so '01' is the misread."},
 {id:"p41m3q3", q:"You prepend qc.x(1) to the Bell circuit qc.h(0); qc.cx(0, 1) and run 4,000 shots on a simulator. What counts do you expect?", options:["About 2,000 each of '00' and '11'","About 1,000 each of all four strings","About 2,000 each of '01' and '10'","All 4,000 shots read '10'"], answer:2, why:"X(1) starts the circuit in |10⟩. H on qubit 0 gives (|10⟩ + |11⟩)/√2, and CX(0,1) flips qubit 1 only in the second term, giving (|10⟩ + |01⟩)/√2, the Bell state Ψ⁺. The bits always disagree, so '00' and '11' never appear."},
 {id:"p41m3q4", q:"A startup claims quantum advantage because its 60-qubit circuit, built only from H, S and CNOT gates, has 2⁶⁰ amplitudes no classical computer could store. What is wrong with the claim?", options:["Clifford-only circuits are efficiently simulable classically","Sixty qubits fit easily in a laptop's statevector memory","H, S and CNOT cannot create any entanglement at all","Advantage needs at least 1,000 physical qubits to count"], answer:0, why:"The Gottesman–Knill theorem says H, S and CNOT circuits are efficiently simulable with the stabilizer formalism; the hardness comes from T gates. These gates do entangle (H plus CNOT makes a Bell state), and 2⁶⁰ amplitudes would need 16 EiB, not a laptop."},
 {id:"p41m3q5", q:"Qubit 0 is prepared in |+⟩ and qubit 1 in |−⟩. You apply CX with qubit 0 as control, then H on qubit 0, and measure qubit 0. What do you read?", options:["0 with certainty, since CNOT never changes its control","1 with certainty, because the phase kicked back","0 or 1 with 50% each, since the qubits are now entangled","0 with certainty, since qubit 1 absorbs the phase"], answer:1, why:"Flipping |−⟩ multiplies it by −1, so CX(|+⟩|−⟩) = |−⟩|−⟩: the phase kicks back onto the control. H then maps |−⟩ to |1⟩. Believing CNOT never touches its control is the misconception, and the output is a product state, not entangled."}
],
"p41m4":[
 {id:"p41m4q1", q:"Grover's search runs over N = 64 candidates with one marked item. How many iterations give the highest success probability?", options:["8","32","6","64"], answer:2, why:"The optimal count is ⌊(π/4)√N⌋ = ⌊0.785 × 8⌋ = ⌊6.28⌋ = 6, giving about 99.7% success. Eight is √N without the π/4 factor, and running extra iterations overshoots: the state rotates past the marked item."},
 {id:"p41m4q2", q:"With N = 4 and one marked item, a single Grover iteration finds it with 100% probability. A student runs two iterations 'to be safe'. What is the success probability now?", options:["25%","100%","94.5%","50%"], answer:0, why:"sin θ = 1/√4 gives θ = 30°. After k iterations the angle is (2k+1)θ: one iteration reaches 90°, two reach 150°, and sin²(150°) = 0.25, no better than guessing. Extra iterations rotate the state past the target."},
 {id:"p41m4q3", q:"Factoring N = 21 with a = 2, the quantum period-finding step returns r = 6. Which classical step correctly finishes the job?", options:["2⁶ ≡ 1, so gcd(63, 21) = 21 and gcd(65, 21) = 1","None: the measurement itself already read out 3 and 7","r is even, so the run fails and a new a must be chosen","2³ = 8, so gcd(7, 21) = 7 and gcd(9, 21) = 3"], answer:3, why:"Use a^(r/2): 2³ = 8, which is not −1 mod 21, so gcd(8 − 1, 21) = 7 and gcd(8 + 1, 21) = 3. Using a^r gives only the trivial factors 21 and 1. The quantum part outputs the period, not the factors, and an even r is what you want."},
 {id:"p41m4q4", q:"You run Deutsch–Jozsa on a promised constant-or-balanced function of 4 bits and measure the input register as '0100'. What do you conclude?", options:["f is constant, since one query was enough","f is balanced: a constant f returns 0000","Nothing yet: repeat until '0000' appears","f(0100) = 1 and every other input gives 0"], answer:1, why:"The amplitude of |0000⟩ is (1/16)·Σ(−1)^f(x): ±1 if f is constant, exactly 0 if balanced. A constant function always returns all zeros, so any other outcome proves f is balanced. Waiting for '0000' is pointless, since a balanced f never produces it."},
 {id:"p41m4q5", q:"A VQE run with finite shots reports −1.8601 hartree for an H₂ Hamiltonian whose exact ground energy is −1.8573 hartree. How should you read the result?", options:["As shot noise or a bug: the ground energy is a hard floor","As success: the optimizer beat exact diagonalization","As normal: VQE energies always approach the ground energy from below","As a sign of quantum advantage over the classical solver"], answer:0, why:"The variational principle guarantees ⟨ψ(θ)|H|ψ(θ)⟩ never falls below the true ground energy, so an estimate under it reflects statistical shot noise or an error in the Hamiltonian or code. VQE approaches the ground energy from above, never from below."}
],
"p41m5":[
 {id:"p41m5q1", q:"A two-qubit gate lasts 84 ns on qubits with T2 = 140 µs, and randomized benchmarking measures its error at 2.0 × 10⁻³. What does the comparison tell you?", options:["Decoherence explains essentially all of it, so only a longer T2 can help","RB must be wrong, since gate error cannot exceed t/T2","Decoherence gives only ~6 × 10⁻⁴; the rest is control, leakage, crosstalk","Most of it is readout error, which randomized benchmarking counts as gate error"], answer:2, why:"t/T2 = 0.084/140 ≈ 6 × 10⁻⁴, under a third of the measured error. The rest comes from control imprecision, leakage, crosstalk and material defects. Randomized benchmarking is designed to separate gate error from preparation and readout error, so readout is not the explanation."},
 {id:"p41m5q2", q:"A three-qubit repetition code protects against bit flips that hit each qubit independently with p = 2%. About how often does majority-vote decoding fail?", options:["About 0.12%","About 6%","About 0.04%","About 0.67%"], answer:0, why:"Decoding fails only when two or three qubits flip: 3p² − 2p³ = 3(0.0004) − 2(0.000008) ≈ 0.00118, about 0.12%, roughly seventeen times better than a bare qubit. Six percent is 3p, which wrongly treats any single flip as fatal."},
 {id:"p41m5q3", q:"A team runs Grover circuits with the Sampler and needs each returned bitstring to be trustworthy. They ask you to enable ZNE. What do you advise?", options:["Enable ZNE: it removes the noise from each individual shot","Use PEC instead: it is unbiased, so every single shot comes out exact","Raise the shot count tenfold, which removes the hardware bias","ZNE only repairs expectation values; only QEC fixes single shots"], answer:3, why:"Mitigation such as ZNE or PEC leaves every shot as noisy as before and corrects averaged expectation values, which is why it belongs to the Estimator. The Sampler offers suppression (DD, twirling); only error correction makes individual runs reliable. More shots shrink statistics, not bias."},
 {id:"p41m5q4", q:"PEC on your circuit has a per-layer overhead γ = 1.03. Using the lecture's γ^(2L) rule, about how many times more shots does a 50-layer circuit need for the same error bar?", options:["About 4.4×","About 19×","About 370×","About 1.06×"], answer:1, why:"γ^(2L) = 1.03¹⁰⁰ ≈ e^2.96 ≈ 19. Forgetting the factor of 2 gives 1.03⁵⁰ ≈ 4.4, and 370 is the 100-layer cost. The exponent grows with circuit size, which is why mitigation is a bridge to error correction, not a destination."},
 {id:"p41m5q5", q:"A rotated surface-code patch has distance d = 9. How many physical qubits does it use, and how many errors per round can it correct?", options:["81 qubits; up to 4 errors","161 qubits; up to 9 errors","161 qubits; up to 4 errors","162 qubits; up to 8 errors"], answer:2, why:"The rotated layout uses d² data qubits plus d² − 1 measure qubits: 81 + 80 = 161. It corrects ⌊(d − 1)/2⌋ = 4 errors per round; d = 9 is the smallest number of errors that can cause an undetected logical failure, not the number it corrects."}
],
"p41m6":[
 {id:"p41m6q1", q:"Your transpiled Bell circuit uses one CZ with error 2.5 × 10⁻³, on qubits whose readout errors are 2.0% and 1.0%. About what share of 4,000 shots should land on the forbidden '01' and '10'?", options:["About 0.25%","About 2.0%","About 6.5%","About 3.2%"], answer:3, why:"P(no error) ≈ 0.9975 × 0.980 × 0.990 ≈ 0.968, so about 3.2% of shots, roughly 130, should be forbidden. Counting only the CZ gives 0.25% and ignores readout, which is most of the error in a short two-qubit circuit."},
 {id:"p41m6q2", q:"The layout puts two interacting qubits four hops apart on a heavy-hex chip. Without swapping back, how many extra CZs does routing add, and at 1.5 × 10⁻³ per CZ how much failure per shot?", options:["3 extra CZs, about 0.45% more failure","9 extra CZs, about 1.3% more failure","4 extra CZs, about 0.6% more failure","12 extra CZs, about 1.8% more failure"], answer:1, why:"Four hops apart needs three SWAPs to make the pair neighbours, and each SWAP costs three CZs: nine extra. 1 − 0.9985⁹ ≈ 1.3%. Counting each SWAP as one gate gives 3; this is why you check count_ops before spending QPU time."},
 {id:"p41m6q3", q:"A Bell circuit run for 100,000 shots shows 3.6% of results in '01' and '10'. A teammate wants a million shots to push that share toward zero. What is the right response?", options:["Shots shrink statistical error, not hardware error; 3.6% stays","Agree: with ten times the shots the forbidden share falls tenfold","Agree: the error falls as 1/√N, so it drops to about 1.1%","Disagree: past 100,000 shots, Sampler results become less precise"], answer:0, why:"More shots estimate the noisy distribution more precisely: the standard error √(p(1−p)/N) shrinks, but the 3.6% comes from readout and gate error and does not move. Applying 1/√N to the error rate itself confuses statistical uncertainty with hardware bias."},
 {id:"p41m6q4", q:"The Estimator rejects your job: your two-qubit SparsePauliOp('ZZ') does not match the 156-qubit ISA circuit the transpiler produced. What is the correct fix?", options:["Rebuild the circuit with 156 qubits before transpiling it","Set resilience_level to 0 so the shape check is skipped","Map the observable with obs.apply_layout(isa.layout)","Switch to the Sampler and compute ZZ from raw counts"], answer:2, why:"Transpiling places the circuit on physical qubits of the full chip, so the observable must be placed on the same qubits with apply_layout. Rebuilding with 156 qubits still leaves ZZ on the wrong positions, and resilience settings do not change shape validation."},
 {id:"p41m6q5", q:"You plan 30 Sampler jobs at about 25 QPU-seconds each on the Open Plan. Do they fit one 28-day window, and what would the same work cost pay-as-you-go at $96 per minute?", options:["Yes, because queue time is free; about $72","No, 750 s exceeds 600 s; about $1,200","Yes, the plan allows 60 minutes; about $1,200","No, 750 s exceeds 600 s; about $72,000"], answer:1, why:"30 × 25 = 750 QPU-seconds, more than the Open Plan's 600 seconds per 28 days. Pay-as-you-go bills per second at $96 per minute: 12.5 minutes × $96 = $1,200. Queue time is free, but QPU time is the budget; $72,000 multiplies seconds by the per-minute price."}
],
"p41m7":[
 {id:"p41m7q1", q:"In a BB84 link, Eve intercepts and resends 40% of the photons in randomly chosen bases. What QBER should Alice and Bob see on the sifted key, and can secret key still be distilled with one-way post-processing?", options:["About 40%; far above any limit, so they abort","About 25%, since any interception gives the full signature","About 10%; under 11%, so a small secret key survives","About 20%; above the 11% limit, so they abort"], answer:2, why:"Intercepting a fraction f adds f/4 errors: 0.40 / 4 = 10%. That is just under the Shor–Preskill 11% limit, where r = 1 − 2h(0.10) ≈ 0.06 secret bits per sifted bit. The 25% signature applies only when Eve intercepts every photon."},
 {id:"p41m7q2", q:"Patient records must stay secret for 12 years. Your migration to post-quantum key exchange will take 4 years, and you estimate a cryptographically relevant quantum computer in 10 years. What does Mosca's inequality say?", options:["At risk now: 12 + 4 = 16 exceeds 10","Safe: the 4-year migration ends before year 10","Safe until year 10, then re-encrypt the archive","At risk only if the machine arrives within 4 years"], answer:0, why:"Mosca: if X + Y > Z, data encrypted today will be read. Here 12 + 4 = 16 > 10, so traffic recorded now is exposed through harvest-now-decrypt-later. Finishing before the machine exists does not protect key exchanges recorded during the four migration years."},
 {id:"p41m7q3", q:"With a limited budget, which post-quantum migration step should come first for an organization's data in transit?", options:["Replace AES-256 file encryption, since Grover halves its strength","Re-sign last year's short-lived TLS certificates with ML-DSA","Wait for a working CRQC demonstration, then migrate all at once","Move TLS and VPN key exchange to hybrid ML-KEM"], answer:3, why:"Harvest-now-decrypt-later targets recorded key exchanges, so key exchange moves first; hybrid ML-KEM is already the default in Chrome and OpenSSH 10. Signatures need only resist forgery when verified, so short-lived certificates can wait, and AES-256 keeps 128-bit security against Grover."},
 {id:"p41m7q4", q:"A client wants QKD over 150 km of dark fiber at 0.2 dB/km and proposes an EDFA at the midpoint to boost the signal. Evaluate the plan.", options:["30 dB of loss (0.1% arrive); the EDFA brings the key rate back","30 dB of loss (0.1% arrive); no-cloning forbids amplifying it","3 dB of loss (50% arrive); no amplifier is needed at all","30 dB of loss (3% arrive); a trusted node would add nothing"], answer:1, why:"150 km × 0.2 dB/km = 30 dB, a factor of 1,000, so 0.1% of photons arrive. An optical amplifier would have to copy unknown quantum states, which no-cloning forbids, so QKD cannot cross EDFAs; long links need trusted nodes or, someday, quantum repeaters."},
 {id:"p41m7q5", q:"Your C1000-179 practice tests average 72% per question; the pass mark is 47 of 68 (69%). What is the soundest decision?", options:["Book now: 72% beats 69%, so a pass is nearly certain","Book now: per-question accuracy does not affect pass odds","Keep studying: at 72% you pass only about 3 times in 4","Keep studying: at 72% you would pass under one time in ten"], answer:2, why:"At 72% on 68 questions the score averages about 49 with a spread of about 3.7, so falling below 47 happens roughly a quarter of the time. The lecture's rule is to book at 85% or above; even 75% passes only about nine times in ten."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p42m1":[
 {id:"p42m1q1", q:"Meshtastic nodes run at 915 MHz. About how long is a quarter-wave vertical whip for that frequency?", options:["About 16 cm","About 8 cm","About 33 cm","About 82 cm"], answer:1, why:"λ = 300/915 ≈ 0.328 m, so a quarter wave is about 8.2 cm. 16 cm is the half-wave length and 33 cm the full wavelength; 82 cm comes from slipping a decimal place. The band tells you the hardware size before you buy anything."},
 {id:"p42m1q2", q:"You turn a 100 W (50 dBm) HF transceiver down to 25 W. What is its output now in dBm?", options:["47 dBm","25 dBm","40 dBm","44 dBm"], answer:3, why:"25 W is a quarter of 100 W, two halvings, so 50 − 3 − 3 = 44 dBm (10 log 25,000 mW ≈ 44). 47 dBm is a single halving to 50 W, 40 dBm is 10 W, and 25 dBm confuses watts with dBm."},
 {id:"p42m1q3", q:"A site survey reads −65 dBm next to the access point and −95 dBm in a far office. How much less power arrives in the far office?", options:["1,000 times less","30 times less","100 times less","About 1.5 times less"], answer:0, why:"The difference is 30 dB, and every 10 dB is a factor of ten: 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000. Reading 30 dB as a factor of 30, or comparing −65 and −95 as plain numbers (about 1.5), treats a logarithmic ratio as linear."},
 {id:"p42m1q4", q:"Your legal 33 cm amateur station at 927 MHz interferes with a neighbor's Part 15 LoRa gateway, and the gateway also disrupts your reception. Under FCC rules, which statement is right?", options:["Both are equal users of an ISM band, so neither of you has priority","You must stop: ISM bands belong to unlicensed devices first","The gateway must accept your signal and must not interfere with you","Whoever installed first gets priority in the band"], answer:2, why:"Part 15 devices operate unlicensed on condition that they accept any interference and cause no harmful interference to licensed users. Your amateur license gives you standing on 902–928 MHz that a Part 15 device never has; unlicensed does not mean first in line."},
 {id:"p42m1q5", q:"A new Technician plans these operations. Which one breaks the license's power or frequency privileges?", options:["FM simplex on 146.520 MHz at 50 W from a car","SSB phone on 28.400 MHz at 500 W PEP","FT8 on 50.313 MHz at 100 W","CW on 7.050 MHz at 150 W"], answer:1, why:"Technicians may use SSB on 28.300–28.500 MHz, but their HF power is capped at 200 W PEP. Above 30 MHz, as on 2 m and 6 m, they may run up to 1,500 W, and 7.050 MHz lies in their 40 m CW segment, within 200 W."}
],
"p42m2":[
 {id:"p42m2q1", q:"An AM transmitter at 100% modulation puts out 150 W in total. How much power is in each sideband?", options:["50 W","75 W","25 W","100 W"], answer:2, why:"At 100% modulation two-thirds of the power, 100 W, sits in the carrier, which carries no information; the remaining 50 W splits between two mirror-image sidebands, 25 W each. That waste is why SSB, which puts all its power in one sideband, won HF voice."},
 {id:"p42m2q2", q:"What is the thermal noise floor of a 200 kHz-wide FM broadcast receiver at 290 K with a 6 dB noise figure?", options:["About −115 dBm","About −121 dBm","About −168 dBm","About −95 dBm"], answer:0, why:"kTB = −174 dBm/Hz + 10 log10(200,000) ≈ −174 + 53 = −121 dBm, and the noise figure adds 6 dB: −115 dBm. −121 dBm forgets the noise figure, and −168 dBm adds the noise figure to the per-hertz density but drops the bandwidth term."},
 {id:"p42m2q3", q:"A 10 MHz-wide channel runs at 20 dB SNR. What is its Shannon capacity?", options:["About 200 Mbps","About 1 Gbps","About 6.7 Mbps","About 67 Mbps"], answer:3, why:"20 dB is S/N = 100, so C = B log2(1 + S/N) = 10 MHz × log2(101) ≈ 10 × 6.66 ≈ 67 Mbps. 200 Mbps multiplies the bandwidth by the dB figure, and 1 Gbps multiplies it by the linear ratio without the logarithm."},
 {id:"p42m2q4", q:"A link keeps its symbol rate but steps up from 64-QAM to 256-QAM. What do you gain, and roughly what does it cost?", options:["4× the bit rate for about 3 dB more SNR","33% more bit rate for about 6 dB more SNR","33% more bit rate at no extra SNR cost","Twice the bit rate for about 6 dB more SNR"], answer:1, why:"64-QAM carries log2(64) = 6 bits per symbol and 256-QAM carries 8, a 33% gain. Packing four times the points closer together costs roughly 3 dB per extra bit, about 6 dB, which is why routers fall back to lower orders at the edge of coverage."},
 {id:"p42m2q5", q:"A newly licensed Technician wants to transmit a digital mode this week. Which choice is within Technician privileges?", options:["FT8 on 14.074 MHz with 50 W","RTTY on 14.085 MHz at 100 W","APRS on 144.390 MHz at 5 W","PSK31 on 14.070 MHz with 20 W"], answer:2, why:"2 m is fully open to Technicians, so APRS on 144.390 MHz is allowed. The 20 m digital frequencies around 14.07–14.09 MHz need a General license; a Technician's HF data privilege is 28.000–28.300 MHz, which includes FT8 on 28.074 MHz."}
],
"p42m3":[
 {id:"p42m3q1", q:"A 6 m dipole cut for 50.1 MHz shows resonance at 48.5 MHz on your NanoVNA. What should you do?", options:["Shorten each leg by about 3%","Lengthen each leg by about 3%","Shorten each leg by about 30%","Nothing: a tuner makes length irrelevant"], answer:0, why:"Resonance below the target means the wire is too long: 50.1/48.5 − 1 ≈ 3.3%, so trim each leg by about that much and sweep again. That is why you cut legs a little long; a tuner only hides the mismatch at the radio."},
 {id:"p42m3q2", q:"A friend's new 2 m antenna shows SWR 1.05 everywhere from 120 to 180 MHz, yet contacts are poor. What is the most likely explanation?", options:["The SWR is ideal, so the fault must be in the radio's receiver","An SWR that low proves unusually high antenna gain","A very low SWR means the antenna is resonant on 2 m","Loss is absorbing the power, so it acts like a dummy load"], answer:3, why:"A match that flat across 60 MHz is the signature of loss, in a lossy antenna or a long lossy feedline, not of a good radiator. A dummy load has perfect SWR and radiates nothing: SWR measures match, not radiation, and a resonant dipole's SWR rises off frequency."},
 {id:"p42m3q3", q:"A 50 W rig feeds 100 ft of RG-8X at 446 MHz, which loses about 8 dB per 100 ft. Roughly how much power reaches the antenna?", options:["About 42 W","About 25 W","About 8 W","About 0.8 W"], answer:2, why:"8 dB is a factor of about 6.3 (10^0.8), so 50 W ÷ 6.3 ≈ 8 W. Subtracting 8 watts mixes dB with watts, and 25 W would be only a 3 dB loss. Shorter runs or LMR-400, at about 2.7 dB per 100 ft, are the fix."},
 {id:"p42m3q4", q:"A 25 W transmitter feeds an antenna rated 6 dBd through 1 dB of feedline loss. What is the EIRP?", options:["About 49 dBm, roughly 80 W","About 51 dBm, roughly 130 W","About 52 dBm, roughly 165 W","About 31 dBm, roughly 1.3 W"], answer:1, why:"25 W is 44 dBm, and 6 dBd is 8.15 dBi: 44 + 8.15 − 1 ≈ 51 dBm, about 130 W. Adding 6 without converting dBd to dBi gives 49 dBm, and 52 dBm forgets the feedline loss."},
 {id:"p42m3q5", q:"A 70 cm link is about 4 dB short of the margin you need. Which single change closes the gap?", options:["Replace a 2 dBi vertical with a 7 dBi 3-element Yagi","Double the transmitter's output power from 25 W to 50 W","Replace a 3 ft RG-58 jumper with 3 ft of LMR-400","Retune the antenna so its SWR falls from 1.5 to 1.1"], answer:0, why:"The Yagi adds about 5 dB of gain, enough on its own. Doubling power buys only 3 dB, a 3 ft jumper loses well under 1 dB at 446 MHz on any cable, and SWR 1.5 reflects just 4%, so a perfect match recovers about 0.2 dB."}
],
"p42m4":[
 {id:"p42m4q1", q:"A handheld at 2 m height wants to reach a repeater whose antenna sits 200 m above the surrounding terrain. Roughly what is the greatest distance at which they can still communicate?", options:["About 58 km","About 6 km","About 120 km","About 64 km"], answer:3, why:"Each antenna has its own radio horizon, 4.12 √h km: about 5.8 km for the handheld and 58 km for the repeater. They communicate roughly when the sum, about 64 km, exceeds the distance; 58 km counts only the repeater's horizon."},
 {id:"p42m4q2", q:"At 2 p.m. on 20 m, stations in Europe report your signal as strong, but a friend 250 km away hears nothing. What is the best explanation?", options:["He is in the skip zone between ground wave and the first hop","The daytime D layer is absorbing 20 m signals headed his way","100 W is simply too little power to cover a 250 km path","A tropospheric duct is carrying your signal right past him"], answer:0, why:"Ground wave on 20 m fades within tens of kilometers, while an F2 hop lands thousands of kilometers away; stations in between, like your friend, sit in the skip zone. The D layer mainly absorbs the lower HF bands, and ducting is a VHF/UHF effect."},
 {id:"p42m4q3", q:"It is 2 a.m. local time in 2029, with solar flux low as Cycle 25 declines. Which band is the best bet for a long-distance HF contact?", options:["10 m","40 m","15 m","2 m"], answer:1, why:"At night the D layer disappears, so 40 m, a classic night band, opens for long-distance work. 15 and 10 m need a strongly ionized daytime F2 layer and high solar flux, and 2 m is mostly line-of-sight VHF."},
 {id:"p42m4q4", q:"A LEO satellite transmitting on 1,296 MHz approaches you with a closing speed of up to 7.5 km/s. At that speed, how far off nominal does its signal arrive?", options:["About 3.2 kHz high","About 32 kHz low","About 32 kHz high","About 320 kHz high"], answer:2, why:"Δf = f·v/c = 1,296 MHz × 7,500 / 300,000,000 ≈ 32.4 kHz, and an approaching source arrives above its nominal frequency, so you start high and step down as it departs. 3.2 kHz and 320 kHz slip a decimal place."},
 {id:"p42m4q5", q:"With identical power and antennas, how much more free-space path loss does a 5,180 MHz Wi-Fi channel suffer than a 2,437 MHz channel over the same distance?", options:["About 6.5 dB","About 3.3 dB","About 2.1 dB","None; path loss depends only on distance"], answer:0, why:"FSPL contains 20 log10 f, so the difference is 20 log10(5,180/2,437) ≈ 20 × 0.327 ≈ 6.5 dB, from the smaller effective aperture at shorter wavelength. 3.3 dB uses 10 log instead of 20 log; walls and weaker diffraction widen the gap further."}
],
"p42m5":[
 {id:"p42m5q1", q:"You need to capture a weak signal 70 dB below a strong local transmitter in the same SDR capture. Which receiver's ADC can span that range in theory?", options:["RTL-SDR Blog V4, 8-bit","HackRF One, 8-bit","SDRplay RSP1B, 14-bit","Any of them, if the sample rate is high enough"], answer:2, why:"Dynamic range is about 6.02N + 1.76 dB: 8 bits give about 50 dB, 14 bits about 86 dB. Only the 14-bit SDRplay spans 70 dB. Sample rate sets captured bandwidth, not dynamic range, which is why strong broadcasters swamp an RTL-SDR."},
 {id:"p42m5q2", q:"A HackRF records 10 MSPS of complex 8-bit samples for 30 seconds. About how large is the file?", options:["About 600 MB","About 300 MB","About 4.8 GB","About 75 MB"], answer:0, why:"Each complex sample is an I byte plus a Q byte: 10 M × 2 × 1 byte = 20 MB/s, and 30 s gives 600 MB. 300 MB forgets the Q channel, and 4.8 GB counts bits as bytes. That is why you record only the slice you need and decimate early."},
 {id:"p42m5q3", q:"In your GNU Radio FM receiver the source runs at 2.0 MSPS and the Low Pass Filter decimates by 8. What quadrature rate must the WBFM Receive block be given?", options:["2.0 MSPS","200 kSPS","16 MSPS","250 kSPS"], answer:3, why:"Decimation divides the rate: 2.0 MSPS ÷ 8 = 250 kSPS, and every block after the filter must be told that rate. 2.0 MSPS ignores the decimation, 16 MSPS multiplies instead of dividing, and 200 kHz is the station's bandwidth, not a sample rate."},
 {id:"p42m5q4", q:"A friend with no amateur license wants to transmit test signals from a HackRF on 915 MHz, reasoning that 902–928 MHz is unlicensed. What is the correct answer?", options:["Allowed: anyone at all may transmit on an unlicensed band","Not allowed: unlicensed use is for certified Part 15 devices","Allowed, provided the output stays below 1 W","Allowed, since 915 MHz is an ISM band rather than an amateur one"], answer:1, why:"Unlicensed operation is a privilege of certified Part 15 products within their power and emission limits; an uncertified transmitter is not legal there either. Without a license covering the frequency, a HackRF is a receiver with an unused transmit port."},
 {id:"p42m5q5", q:"A teammate lawfully monitors unencrypted county fire dispatch and wants to post clips of callers' medical details on social media. What is the right call?", options:["Fine: anything lawful to receive is lawful to publish","Fine, as long as each clip is shorter than a minute","Don't: Section 705 bars divulging what you intercept","Only a problem if the dispatch was digital P25 traffic"], answer:2, why:"Receiving unencrypted public-safety traffic is lawful federally, but Section 705 forbids divulging or using intercepted contents except broadcasts, amateur, CB and transmissions for the general public. Lawful listening does not make publishing other people's communications lawful."}
],
"p42m6":[
 {id:"p42m6q1", q:"A repeater contact runs for 23 minutes. To meet Part 97's identification rule, when must you give your call sign at minimum?", options:["At 10 and 20 minutes and at the end","Only once, at the start","Only once, at the end of the contact","At the start and then every 30 minutes"], answer:0, why:"97.119 requires identification at least every 10 minutes during a contact and at its end, so at about 10 and 20 minutes and again when you sign off at 23. Identifying only once, at either end, leaves gaps longer than ten minutes."},
 {id:"p42m6q2", q:"RepeaterBook lists a repeater with output 147.180 MHz, a plus offset and tone 103.5 Hz. What should your handheld transmit?", options:["146.580 MHz with a 103.5 Hz tone","147.180 MHz with a 103.5 Hz tone","147.780 MHz with a 103.5 Hz tone","147.780 MHz with no tone at all"], answer:2, why:"On 2 m the standard offset is 600 kHz, and a plus offset means you transmit on 147.180 + 0.600 = 147.780 MHz while listening on the output. The repeater wants the CTCSS tone on its input, so the tone rides on your transmission."},
 {id:"p42m6q3", q:"A club wants to run its event communications over DMR on 70 cm with AES encryption so the public cannot listen. What do you tell them?", options:["Fine, as long as every station still identifies","Not allowed: Part 97 forbids obscuring meaning","Fine on DMR, since the standard is commercial","Allowed only on simplex, never through repeaters"], answer:1, why:"97.113 prohibits codes and ciphers intended to obscure meaning on amateur frequencies, with narrow exceptions such as space-station control. Identifying does not cure it; private traffic belongs on Part 15 devices or commercial services, not ham bands."},
 {id:"p42m6q4", q:"As a brand-new candidate you pass Elements 2 and 3 at the same Saturday session. When may you first transmit, and with which privileges?", options:["Right away on HF, by adding /AG to your name","Right away on 2 m only, on the strength of the CSCE","Ten days later, once the $35 fee has been paid","Once your call sign shows in ULS, as a General"], answer:3, why:"A new licensee may transmit only after the grant appears in ULS, and passing both elements earns a General license. /AG is for existing licensees operating on an upgrade before ULS updates, and paying the fee lets the grant proceed but is not the grant."},
 {id:"p42m6q5", q:"Your unlicensed brother wants to talk over your station, with you as control operator, to (a) a ham in Ohio and (b) a ham in a country with no third-party agreement with the US. Which is permitted?", options:["Both, since you are the control operator","Only the contact with the ham in Ohio","Neither, since he holds no license","Only the contact with the foreign ham"], answer:1, why:"Third-party traffic for friends and family is fine domestically with a licensed control operator in charge, but internationally it needs a third-party agreement with that country (97.115). Being the control operator does not make up for the missing agreement."}
],
"p42m7":[
 {id:"p42m7q1", q:"A warehouse's wireless alarm sensors keep losing supervision. Receivers show high RSSI but climbing packet errors, and the waterfall shows a broad, flat hump lifting the noise floor about 30 dB. What is your assessment and response?", options:["Weak signal: raise the sensors' transmit power","Replay attack: switch the sensors to rolling codes","Rogue AP: contain it with WIPS deauthentication","Jamming: wired fallback, jam alerts, report to FCC"], answer:3, why:"A featureless hump that raises the noise floor while RSSI stays high is the jamming signature. The defenses are wired fallback for critical links, jam-detection alerts and a report to the FCC, never transmitting back. Raising sensor power does not restore a swamped receiver."},
 {id:"p42m7q2", q:"Logs show a client's 315 MHz gate opener accepting duplicate frames at 3 a.m. Its remotes send a fixed code. Which change addresses the weakness?", options:["Rolling-code or challenge–response remotes","A longer, encrypted fixed code in each remote","A more sensitive receiver at the gate","Moving the system to 433.92 MHz"], answer:0, why:"Duplicate frames point to replay. A rolling code with a window, or challenge–response, makes each valid transmission usable only once. Encrypting a fixed code changes nothing, because the recorded ciphertext is exactly what the receiver accepts; frequency and sensitivity are irrelevant."},
 {id:"p42m7q3", q:"A fleet tracker shows a truck jump 40 km in two seconds, and its GPS and Galileo positions disagree. What is the likeliest cause and the right engineering defense?", options:["Multipath: move the GNSS antenna higher on the cab","Jamming: add a stronger GPS amplifier to overpower it","Spoofing: multi-constellation checks and Galileo OSNMA","Eavesdropping: encrypt the tracker's uplink with TLS"], answer:2, why:"Impossible velocities and disagreeing constellations are the spoofing signature, because GPS C/A has no sender authentication. Multi-constellation, multi-frequency receivers with RAIM and Galileo's authenticated OSNMA signal detect it. Jamming makes receivers lose lock rather than report false positions."},
 {id:"p42m7q4", q:"A voice-over-Wi-Fi survey point reads −65 dBm from the best AP with a noise floor of −88 dBm. Against the usual voice targets, does it pass?", options:["Passes: −65 dBm beats the −67 dBm coverage target","Fails: coverage passes, but SNR is only 23 dB","Fails: −65 dBm is weaker than the −67 dBm target","Passes: the SNR works out to 153 dB"], answer:1, why:"Voice needs −67 dBm or better and an SNR of at least 25 dB. −65 dBm is stronger than −67 dBm, so coverage passes, but −65 − (−88) = 23 dB falls short. Adding the magnitudes gives the meaningless 153 dB."},
 {id:"p42m7q5", q:"Users on a WPA2-Personal network are repeatedly knocked offline by forged deauthentication frames. Which defensive change ends this?", options:["A longer, more complex WPA2 passphrase","Hiding the SSID from beacon frames","Raising AP transmit power to overpower the attacker","WPA3 with Protected Management Frames (802.11w)"], answer:3, why:"Deauthentication frames are management frames, which WPA2 networks without 802.11w do not authenticate. WPA3 makes PMF mandatory, so forged deauths are rejected. A passphrase protects data frames, not management frames, and a hidden SSID still shows up in probe traffic."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p43m1":[
 {id:"p43m1q1", q:"You want to run a 100B dense model quantized to 4 bits per weight, assuming the GPU can wire about three-quarters of unified memory. Which machine can hold it, and what is its rough decode ceiling?", options:["Mac Studio M5 Max, 128 GB: about 12 tokens/s","Mac mini M5 Pro, 64 GB: about 6 tokens/s at best","RTX 5090 PC, 32 GB: about 36 tokens/s","Mac Studio M5 Max, 128 GB: about 6 tokens/s"], answer:0, why:"Weights ≈ 100 × 4 ÷ 8 = 50 GB. A 64 GB Mac lets the GPU wire about 48 GB and a 32 GB card holds less, so only the 128 GB Mac fits; its ceiling is 614 GB/s ÷ 50 GB ≈ 12 tokens/s. 6 tokens/s divides by 100 GB, the 8-bit size."},
 {id:"p43m1q2", q:"A 30B model at 4 bits (about 15 GB) runs on an M5 Pro with 64 GB and on an M5 Max with 128 GB. What should you expect?", options:["Equal speed, since both machines hold 15 GB with room to spare","M5 Max about twice as fast, because its bandwidth is double","M5 Max faster, because double the memory doubles the speed","M5 Pro faster, because a smaller memory pool has less latency"], answer:1, why:"Capacity decides what fits; bandwidth decides decode speed. The ceilings are about 307 ÷ 15 ≈ 20 and 614 ÷ 15 ≈ 41 tokens/s, so the M5 Max is roughly twice as fast because of its 614 GB/s, not its extra 64 GB, which this model never touches."},
 {id:"p43m1q3", q:"A script written on Linux runs sed -i 's/old/new/' config.txt on a Mac, and sed fails with an invalid command error. What is the fix?", options:["Run the script with sudo so sed can write the file","Change the shebang to #!/bin/bash so the GNU tools are used","Add -E so sed uses extended regular expressions","Pass BSD sed an empty suffix: sed -i '' 's/old/new/'"], answer:3, why:"macOS ships BSD sed, where -i takes a backup suffix as its next argument, so the expression is swallowed as the suffix and the filename is parsed as a command. sed -i '' supplies an empty suffix. Changing the shell does not change which sed runs."},
 {id:"p43m1q4", q:"A user says an internal tool downloaded from a vendor's site 'must be safe because it is notarized'. What does notarization actually establish?", options:["Apple reviewed its features and privacy like an App Store app","The vendor's code was audited by Apple for security bugs","That exact build passed Apple's automated malware scan","The app runs in the same sandbox as iOS App Store apps"], answer:2, why:"Notarization is an automated malware scan and signature check of a specific build distributed outside the App Store, and Gatekeeper verifies its ticket. It is not a quality, privacy or code review, so it lowers malware risk without proving the tool is well behaved."},
 {id:"p43m1q5", q:"Your next lab machine must train models with CUDA (Domain 9) and run x86 malware-analysis VMs (Domains 8 and 11). What is the honest recommendation?", options:["A Mac Studio with 512 GB, since unified memory beats everything","A PC with an NVIDIA GPU, adding a Mac later only if needed","A MacBook Neo, since MLX and Rosetta cover CUDA and x86","Any Mac, since ARM VMs run x86 security tools natively"], answer:1, why:"CUDA does not exist on Macs (MLX is the alternative), and Apple silicon runs ARM VMs, so x86 tools are only emulated. A PC with NVIDIA wins both needs; a Mac earns its place for very large local models or Mac administration, not here."}
],
"p43m2":[
 {id:"p43m2q1", q:"An admin account created with sysadminctl after setup cannot authorize a software update on an Apple silicon Mac, although it is in the admin group. What is the likely cause?", options:["It must also be added to the wheel group","SIP blocks admins created after Setup Assistant","It has no secure token, so it is not a volume owner","Its UID is below 501, which marks it as a system account"], answer:2, why:"On Apple silicon only secure-token holders, the volume owners, can authorize updates and startup-security changes. Accounts made after setup receive a token from an existing holder or through the MDM-escrowed bootstrap token; group membership alone is not enough."},
 {id:"p43m2q2", q:"A LaunchAgent's plist sets ProgramArguments to ~/bin/backup.sh. launchctl print shows a nonzero last exit code, and the script never runs. What is the bug?", options:["launchd won't expand ~; give an absolute path","Agents must live in /System/Library/LaunchAgents","StartInterval is ignored unless KeepAlive is true","The plist must be converted to binary with plutil"], answer:0, why:"launchd passes ProgramArguments literally, with no tilde expansion, so ~/bin/backup.sh is not a valid path; write /Users/name/bin/backup.sh. /System is sealed and off limits, and launchd reads XML plists perfectly well."},
 {id:"p43m2q3", q:"An agent runs every hour with StartInterval 3600. A MacBook sleeps from 1 a.m. to 7 a.m. What happens when it wakes?", options:["Six runs fire back to back to catch up","Nothing until the next full hour on the clock","Interval jobs stay paused until the next reboot","The missed runs coalesce into a single run"], answer:3, why:"launchd coalesces intervals missed during sleep into one run on wake, then resumes the schedule. It does not replay every missed interval and needs no reboot; this behavior is one reason launchd, not cron, is the native scheduler."},
 {id:"p43m2q4", q:"On macOS 15 or later a user cannot open an unnotarized internal tool, and Control-clicking Open no longer works. What is the correct path?", options:["Disable SIP from recoveryOS so the tool can launch","Approve it once with Open Anyway in Privacy & Security","Run the app binary with sudo to bypass Gatekeeper","Turn Gatekeeper off fleet-wide with spctl --master-disable"], answer:1, why:"Since Sequoia the Control-click bypass is gone: a user who trusts an unnotarized app approves it once under Privacy & Security with Open Anyway, and the lasting fix is to get it signed and notarized. SIP is unrelated, and disabling Gatekeeper weakens every Mac."},
 {id:"p43m2q5", q:"You run defaults write com.apple.screensaver idleTime -int 600, but the setting keeps returning to 300. What is the most likely reason?", options:["You forgot to run killall Dock after writing","defaults cannot write binary property lists","An MDM-managed preference is overriding it","SIP protects every preference file from edits"], answer:2, why:"Settings pushed by MDM land in /Library/Managed Preferences and override the user's value, so the profile is what must change. defaults reads and writes binary plists fine, killall Dock only reloads the Dock, and SIP protects system locations, not user preferences."}
],
"p43m3":[
 {id:"p43m3q1", q:"After two failed reinstalls, an Apple silicon Mac no longer shows startup options at all, and the user's data has no recent backup. What is the next step?", options:["DFU restore from a second Mac, then rebuild from scratch","DFU revive from a second Mac, keeping the data","Reset NVRAM and the SMC, then try the reinstall again","Erase All Content and Settings from the login window"], answer:1, why:"With startup options gone, firmware or recoveryOS is damaged, and a DFU revive from Apple Configurator on a second Mac reinstalls both without erasing data. Restore erases everything, so it comes only if revive fails; NVRAM and SMC resets are Intel-era fixes."},
 {id:"p43m3q2", q:"On a Mac, ping 1.1.1.1 succeeds but ping apple.com returns 'cannot resolve host', and every website fails. What is the best next step?", options:["Rejoin Wi-Fi, since the radio link has dropped","Run Apple Diagnostics to test the Wi-Fi hardware","Open TCP 443 to 17.0.0.0/8 on the firewall","Check scutil --dns and flush the DNS cache"], answer:3, why:"Reaching an IP address proves the link and routing work, so failing names isolates DNS. scutil --dns shows the resolvers (a VPN, filter or relay setting may have changed them), and flushing the cache clears stale entries. The Wi-Fi link and hardware are already proven."},
 {id:"p43m3q3", q:"For a ticket you need every error-level message mdmclient logged in the last 30 minutes. Which command gets it?", options:["log show --last 30m --predicate 'process == \"mdmclient\" AND messageType == error'","log stream --predicate 'process == \"mdmclient\" AND messageType == error'","grep mdmclient /var/log/system.log | grep -i error","log show --last 30m --predicate 'subsystem == \"mdmclient\"'"], answer:0, why:"log show reads stored history over a time window, and the predicate filters by process and level. log stream shows only new messages from now on, the unified log lives in tracev3 files that grep cannot read, and mdmclient is a process name, not a subsystem."},
 {id:"p43m3q4", q:"Since a new docking-station driver was installed, a Mac panics within minutes of every boot. What is the least destructive route to a fix?", options:["Erase the Mac and restore it from Time Machine","DFU restore it, since a panic loop means failed firmware","Read the panic backtrace, boot Safe Mode, remove it","Reset NVRAM and the SMC from the keyboard"], answer:2, why:"The .panic report in /Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports usually names the guilty extension, and Safe Mode skips third-party extensions so you can remove it. Erasing and restoring destroy work the fix does not need, and Apple silicon has no user-resettable SMC."},
 {id:"p43m3q5", q:"A departed employee's FileVault Mac arrives with no known password, and its recovery key was never escrowed. The manager wants the files. What do you tell them?", options:["Apple can unlock it once you show proof of purchase","It is unrecoverable by design; erase and redeploy","Share Disk from recoveryOS will copy the files off","Reinstalling macOS will reset the login password"], answer:1, why:"Without a secure-token user's password or the recovery key, nobody can unlock FileVault's keys, which is the point of encryption. Share Disk and a reinstall both leave the Data volume encrypted. The lesson for the fleet is mandatory recovery-key escrow."}
],
"p43m4":[
 {id:"p43m4q1", q:"The MDM push certificate expires next week. Its creator has left and his Apple Account is gone, so a colleague makes a new certificate under her own account. What happens?", options:["Nothing visible; devices accept any valid Apple push certificate","Devices keep working until the old certificate's expiry date","Only iPhones are affected; Macs check in over HTTPS anyway","The push topic changes, so every device must re-enroll"], answer:3, why:"The certificate's topic is tied to the Apple Account that created it, so a new account means a new topic, and devices enrolled under the old one never receive pushes again. Renew from the same account, which is why it should belong to a shared role, not a person."},
 {id:"p43m4q2", q:"MDM commands sit as Pending for every device on a new guest VLAN, while the same devices respond instantly on the staff network. What is the likeliest cause?", options:["The VLAN blocks APNs, so no wake-up arrives","The server cannot open connections into that VLAN","The push certificate expired for that VLAN only","Those devices must re-enroll with User Enrollment"], answer:0, why:"The server never connects to devices. APNs carries a wake-up over the device's persistent connection on TCP 5223 or 443 to Apple's 17.0.0.0/8, and the device then checks in. Block that path and commands queue forever; certificates are not per network."},
 {id:"p43m4q3", q:"A school bought 50 iPads from a marketplace seller outside Apple's channels and wants them supervised. Which enrollment fits?", options:["Automated Device Enrollment, as they will appear automatically","Profile-based enrollment, which supervises them for good","Apple Configurator, removable for a 30-day provisional period","Account-driven User Enrollment with Managed Apple Accounts"], answer:2, why:"Devices bought outside Apple, Authorized Reseller or carrier channels never appear in Apple School Manager by serial, so ADE cannot see them. Apple Configurator adds and supervises them, with a 30-day provisional period during which enrollment can still be removed. Profile-based and User Enrollment are unsupervised."},
 {id:"p43m4q4", q:"An employee asks what IT will be able to see if she enrolls her personal iPhone with account-driven User Enrollment. What is accurate?", options:["Everything on the phone, though IT policy promises not to look","Only the work volume and managed apps, never personal data","Her messages and browsing history, but not her photos","Her location at all times, plus a list of every installed app"], answer:1, why:"User Enrollment keeps work data on a separate cryptographic volume tied to a Managed Apple Account. The organization cannot see the serial number, personal apps or anything outside that volume, and it can remove only the work data: the privacy-preserving path for BYOD."},
 {id:"p43m4q5", q:"A school wants to install a paid app on 200 shared iPads whose students have no Apple Accounts. Which licensing approach works?", options:["Device-based Apps and Books licenses assigned to serials","Each student buys the app with a personal Apple Account","One license installed on all 200 iPads by MDM","User-based licenses tied to the IT admin's Apple Account"], answer:0, why:"With device-based assignment the MDM gives each license to a serial number, so no Apple Account is needed, and licenses can be revoked and reused when iPads are retired. One license cannot cover 200 devices, and personal or admin accounts break shared use."}
],
"p43m5":[
 {id:"p43m5q1", q:"A user forgot her password on a FileVault-enforced Mac whose MDM holds both an escrowed recovery key and a bootstrap token. How does the help desk get her in?", options:["Send the bootstrap token from MDM to unlock the disk","Boot recoveryOS and disable SIP to reset the password","Give her the escrowed recovery key, then rotate it","Erase the Mac, since FileVault has no recovery path"], answer:2, why:"The personal recovery key escrowed in MDM unlocks FileVault, after which MDM issues a new key so the disclosed one is retired. The bootstrap token grants secure tokens and authorizes updates on Apple silicon; it does not unlock FileVault."},
 {id:"p43m5q2", q:"A clinic loses 6 of its 400 laptops in a year. Five were FileVault-encrypted; one was not and held records of 650 patients, 520 of them in one state. What does HIPAA require?", options:["Notify the patients, HHS and the media within 60 days","Nothing, since only one of the six laptops was unencrypted","Report all six laptops to HHS as breaches within 60 days","Notify HHS only, and patients only if misuse is proven"], answer:0, why:"Properly encrypted data is not unsecured health information, so the five FileVault laptops are not reportable. The unencrypted one is a breach of 650 records: notify each person and HHS within 60 days, and the media because more than 500 residents of one state are affected."},
 {id:"p43m5q3", q:"An mSCP scan shows 94 of 100 CIS Level 1 rules passing. The six failures are five cosmetic settings and FileVault disabled on 30 Macs. How should you report it?", options:["94% compliant, so overall risk is low and no action is needed","Fail the whole fleet until all 100 rules pass","Drop the 30 Macs from scope so the score looks better","94% pass, but FileVault-off is critical and goes first"], answer:3, why:"A score counts rules, not risk. One failed critical control, unencrypted Macs, outweighs five cosmetic misses, so rank failures by risk, fix or document each with an owner and a date, and re-scan. Removing failures from the denominator would make the report dishonest."},
 {id:"p43m5q4", q:"A DDM enforcement declaration targets macOS 27.0.1 with a deadline on day 10. By day 4, 55% of 1,500 Macs have updated. What should you expect next?", options:["The other 675 stay behind until each user chooses to update","Most of the other 675 install at the deadline","All 1,500 Macs are forced to update immediately on day 5","No Mac updates after day 4 unless a new declaration is sent"], answer:1, why:"Users install when convenient until the deadline, then each Mac installs on its own at the local deadline. So most of the remaining 45%, 675 Macs, update on day 10, and the stragglers are Macs that are offline, which reports track separately."},
 {id:"p43m5q5", q:"A user says a website told her to paste a Terminal command to 'fix' a download, after which a password dialog appeared. What is the right response?", options:["Infostealer: isolate the Mac, check EDR, rotate passwords","Nothing: Gatekeeper blocks anything pasted into Terminal","Reinstall macOS; that alone removes the risk to her accounts","Disable Gatekeeper so genuine installers stop being blocked"], answer:0, why:"Paste-this-command lures followed by a fake password prompt are the infostealer pattern; they harvest keychains, browser data and wallets. Isolate the Mac, investigate with EDR and rotate the exposed credentials. Gatekeeper does not check pasted commands, and a reinstall does not un-steal passwords."}
],
"p43m6":[
 {id:"p43m6q1", q:"An iPhone on a call is associated at −72 dBm, and a neighboring AP on the same SSID is heard at −66 dBm. Under Apple's published rules, will it roam?", options:["Yes: once below −70 dBm it joins any stronger AP","No: iPhones start looking only below −75 dBm","Yes: the 8 dB rule applies only to idle iPhones","No: it is looking, but the gain is only 6 dB"], answer:3, why:"Below −70 dBm an iPhone starts looking, but while transmitting a candidate must be 8 dB stronger, and −66 is only 6 dB above −72. When idle the bar is 12 dB, and −75 dBm is the Mac's threshold, not the iPhone's."},
 {id:"p43m6q2", q:"150 iPads each need a 6 GB update over a 500 Mb/s branch link. Roughly how long does the WAN stay saturated without a cache, and with one wired content cache?", options:["About 30 minutes, so a cache adds little","About 4 hours, falling to about 2 minutes","About 4 hours, since a cache cannot serve iPads","About 16 hours, falling to about 1 hour"], answer:1, why:"150 × 6 GB = 900 GB = 7,200 Gb; at 0.5 Gb/s that is 14,400 s, about 4 hours. With a cache the WAN carries one 6 GB copy, about 96 s, and the LAN serves the rest. 30 minutes forgets to convert bytes to bits."},
 {id:"p43m6q3", q:"Staff on VLAN 10 must discover AirPrint printers on VLAN 40, while the guest VLAN 50 must not see them. What do you configure?", options:["An mDNS gateway passing only _ipp._tcp from VLAN 40 to 10","An mDNS reflector copying every service between all VLANs","An IP helper forwarding UDP 5353 to the printers' subnet","A firewall rule blocking UDP 5353 on every VLAN"], answer:0, why:"mDNS is link-local multicast that routers never forward, so a gateway copies only the allowed service type between chosen VLANs. Reflecting everything leaks device names and multiplies chatter, IP helpers relay broadcasts such as DHCP rather than multicast discovery, and blocking 5353 breaks AirPrint."},
 {id:"p43m6q4", q:"A consultant asks you to open TCP 22 and 5900 on the office firewall so he can reach the Macs from home. What do you do?", options:["Open both ports, since SSH and VNC are encrypted","Open only 5900, as Screen Sharing needs a password","Keep both closed; give him VPN or overlay access","Open them, but move SSH to a high port to hide it"], answer:2, why:"Remote Login, Screen Sharing and ARD belong behind a VPN or overlay network, never exposed to the internet, where they draw constant password attacks. A password or a non-standard port does not change that; authenticated network access with least exposure does."},
 {id:"p43m6q5", q:"Mobile accounts on AD-bound Macs keep drifting out of sync with users' AD passwords, especially for remote staff. What is the modern fix?", options:["Rebind every Mac with dsconfigad on a monthly schedule","Unbind, then use Platform SSO and Kerberos SSO","Extend the AD machine-password lifetime to 10 years","Require staff to connect to the office VPN at login"], answer:1, why:"Binding is the legacy path: mobile accounts drift and machine passwords expire off-network. Platform SSO ties the Mac login to Entra ID or Okta, and the Kerberos SSO extension fetches on-premises tickets and handles password changes, with no binding."}
],
"p43m7":[
 {id:"p43m7q1", q:"The lecture's Python loop pages through Jamf inventory at page-size 100 and stops when a page returns fewer than 100 rows. With exactly 2,400 computers, how many inventory requests does it make?", options:["24, pages 0 to 23","23, pages 1 to 23","25, the last one empty","2,400, one per computer"], answer:2, why:"Pages 0 to 23 each return a full 100 rows, so the loop cannot tell it is done and requests page 24, which returns 0 rows and ends it: 25 requests. Whenever the total is an exact multiple of the page size, this loop makes one empty request."},
 {id:"p43m7q2", q:"A Jamf policy script reads the app name from $1 and receives '/' instead. What is wrong?", options:["$1 is the mount point; custom values start at $4","Policy parameters reach only Python, not zsh","The admin left the parameter blank in the policy","$1 is the username; the app name arrives in $2"], answer:0, why:"Jamf reserves $1 for the target mount point, $2 for the computer name and $3 for the username; the admin's values arrive in $4 to $11. '/' is exactly the mount point, so the script read the wrong variable."},
 {id:"p43m7q3", q:"The nightly AutoPkg run stops: verify-trust-info reports that a parent recipe's hash has changed. What is the right response?", options:["Run the parent recipe directly, skipping your override","Turn off code-signature verification for that recipe","Delete the trust info so future runs never stop","Review the upstream diff, then update trust if benign"], answer:3, why:"Trust info pins every parent recipe's hash because recipes are code from strangers. A change stops the run until you read the diff; if it is benign, update-trust-info records the new hash. Bypassing the override or the checks removes the protection the pipeline exists to provide."},
 {id:"p43m7q4", q:"You are writing a weekly inventory-report script against the Jamf Pro API. How should it authenticate?", options:["A full-admin API client, so permissions never break it","A read-only API client, secret kept in the Keychain","Your own username and password stored in the script","Classic API basic auth with a shared service account"], answer:1, why:"Least privilege means an API client tied to a role with only Read Computers, exchanging its ID and secret at /api/oauth/token, with the secret in the Keychain or a CI store. A username and password can now only fetch a token, and credentials in a script leak with it."},
 {id:"p43m7q5", q:"Jamf lists 2,600 Macs. 120 have not checked in for 30 days, and 2,410 of the rest run the target OS. What compliance figure belongs in the report?", options:["About 97.2% of 2,480 in scope, stale Macs listed apart","About 92.7%, counting all 2,600 Macs in the denominator","100%, because stale Macs simply do not count at all","About 97.2% of all 2,600 Macs in the inventory"], answer:0, why:"In scope is 2,600 − 120 = 2,480 Macs, and 2,410 ÷ 2,480 ≈ 97.2%. The 120 stale Macs go on a follow-up list with owners rather than being silently dropped or counted as failures; 92.7% divides by all 2,600."}
]
});
